00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:00.600
So we're introducing
sole-tenant nodes

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.130
for Google Compute Engine.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.280
So basically this is
cool because generally,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.720
when you're using
Compute Engine,

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.011
we're essentially multi-tenant.

00:05:08.011 --> 00:05:09.440
So there's a lovely
visualization

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.981
that sits on the
blog post that we'll

00:05:10.981 --> 00:05:12.870
link to about how
basically you can have

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.065
multiple VMs on a
single physical machine,

00:05:15.065 --> 00:05:17.190
and we manage all that for
you with live migrations

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:18.315
and all that kind of stuff.

00:05:18.315 --> 00:05:21.619
Sometimes though that's not a
ideal scenario for whatever it

00:05:21.619 --> 00:05:22.410
is you're building.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.600
Maybe there's certain
compliance and regulation

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.206
that you're required to comply
with that means that you have

00:05:27.206 --> 00:05:28.830
to be on a physical
machine that's only

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.287
and solely operated by you,
or solely tenanted by you,

00:05:31.287 --> 00:05:32.370
and that might be a thing.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.450
Or maybe you run a workload
that is super fine-grained,

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:39.060
so any kind of noisy
neighbor type situation

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.530
at a very, very
low level could be

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:41.610
potentially a problem for you.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.080
I'm thinking particularly
in the gaming space

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.620
if you run dedicated
game servers.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.470
I'm looking at all of you folks.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.410
So yeah, but we're excited to
announce the beta availability

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.010
of sole-tenant nodes.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.160
So basically you're the
only one on the hardware.

