00:06:52.975 --> 00:06:55.350
MELANIE WARRICK: You're very
passionate about this space.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:56.058
MANDY CHAN: Yeah.

00:06:56.058 --> 00:06:59.460
And I've built many
voice applications,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.950
and doing the process
of building those voice

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:06.300
applications I got
frustrated with SSML.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.490
And if you don't
know what SSML is,

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.820
SSML is a Speech
Synthesis Markup Language.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.790
I created an open-source
project called SSML-Builder

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:19.420
to help the voice developers
to make building SSML easier.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:21.630
So that's who I am.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:26.670
And now that I'm at Google, I am
the developer community manager

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.370
for the Action on Google team.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.470
And my role is to engage
with the developers

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:35.050
by creating practical
tools and content.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.560
Basically, improving
the developer experience

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.360
is my goal.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.740
Recently, I worked with my team
to build two Codelabs, level

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.360
one and level two.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.390
So feel free to try that
and give us feedback.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:49.890
MELANIE WARRICK:
So for those who've

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.830
never worked with
SSML, what exactly

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.341
is Speech (GARBLED) Synthesis?

00:07:54.341 --> 00:07:54.840
[HONK]

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.254
Uh, I can't say the
word, but I'm close.

00:07:57.254 --> 00:07:58.670
Anyways, Speech
(GARBLED) Synthe--

00:07:58.670 --> 00:07:59.520
[HONK]

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.110
yeah, this is going
to be a fun one.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.720
Markup language, what
exactly does that mean?

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.330
What does SSML actually
mean, specifically

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.220
for somebody who's
never used it before?

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:11.280
MANDY CHAN: A metaphor that I
like to give to people who are

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.910
technical or people who might
not be technical is basically--

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:18.390
for people who are technical,
it's a markup XML--

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.700
Extensible Markup Language.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.080
And if you are web
developers and you know HTML,

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.720
HTML is the markup
language for the browser.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.100
You markup the text.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:33.690
And for SSML, it is
the markup language

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.750
to give style to the voice.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.480
And I like to say that
the synthesizer is

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:40.890
like the browser for HTML.

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.140
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:43.770
That's very helpful.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:46.470
MARK MANDEL: So what kind of
things can you do with SSML?

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.590
MANDY CHAN: You can do a
lot of things with SSML.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:53.400
So when we speak,
sometimes we pause.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:58.380
So if you want the smart system
to take a break and pause--

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:02.470
sometimes pause can mean
hesitations, suspensions.

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.830
So you can put a
brake tag to pause.

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:08.400
And you can also, for
a number like 625,

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:14.460
it can be June 25th as a
date, or it can be fraction.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:17.190
There's many different
ways to express numbers.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:21.990
And also one of my favorite
SSML tag is called Prosody.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:26.670
And you can change your voice
by manipulating, adjusting

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.640
the pitch, the
volume, and the speed.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:33.030
So I know that in this
space we emphasize

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:36.240
on giving a persona for
your voice application.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.670
And this is how SSML can
help you create a persona.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:41.670
MARK MANDEL: I think you
have a demo, don't you?

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:42.630
MANDY CHAN: Yes, I do.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:43.210
I do.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:47.340
So let me show you guys
how we can create a persona

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.680
by adjusting the Prosody tag.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.164
MELANIE WARRICK: And this
is Action on Google, right?

00:09:52.164 --> 00:09:52.830
MANDY CHAN: Yes.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:56.630
I'm actually just playing
the SSML tag in our Action

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.730
on Google console simulator.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.730
So this Action on
Google is the platform

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:04.850
that we provide for the
third party developers

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.390
to build action for
the Google Assistant.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:09.830
And what is action?

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:13.900
Action is-- I like to use
another metaphor-- it's

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:18.470
the verb that helps people get
things done, turn on the light,

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:21.590
you turn on the TV,
all of these are verbs.

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:25.280
And Action makes our
life more productive

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:27.740
and just makes your
everyday life easier.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:33.260
And let me now demo SSML in the
Action on Google simulator--

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:34.992
how powerful that is.

00:10:34.992 --> 00:10:35.950
AUTOMATED VOICE: Hello.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:37.270
[BOING]

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:40.807
With SSML you can make
speech fast or make it slow.

00:10:40.807 --> 00:10:42.890
(HIGH PITCHED) You can
make it high. (LOW PITCHED)

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.460
Or you can make it low.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:47.110
You can make it loud. (QUIETLY)
Or you can make it quiet.

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:48.680
Or all three things at once.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:51.520
But first, you can make me
spell colors like blue--

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:54.540
B-L-U-E. Edit this
to try yourself.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:57.920
MANDY CHAN: So this is
just a very basic demo

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:02.630
of how SSML can give your voice
application a personality.

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:05.060
And if we add some audio
tech, like for Action

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.640
on Google Platform we
have a sound library that

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:11.990
has all kinds of fun sounds,
like animal sounds or windows

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:13.760
cracking sounds,
different sounds

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.340
that you can add to
your voice applications.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:19.370
And for movie, if you
don't have background music

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:20.360
it won't be the same.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:22.280
And same thing for
the voice application.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:24.800
You want to add the
atmosphere, the mood,

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.561
and give the app
some personality.

00:11:27.561 --> 00:11:29.310
MELANIE WARRICK: So
you can do this with--

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:30.770
you said, with
Actions on Google.

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:33.960
And this can be used for any
voice application, so anything.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.770
It could be used with Assistant,
it could be used with--

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:38.320
MANDY CHAN: Other
platforms, too.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:38.942
Yes.

00:11:38.942 --> 00:11:40.400
MARK MANDEL: So
what sort of things

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.400
are you seeing people do
with these voice actions?

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:44.780
MANDY CHAN: Many
developers, they

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:47.660
have built actions for games.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.270
That's a pretty
popular category.

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:53.400
And they also build for
the home automations.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.370
I'm still looking
forward to what's

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:58.170
possible that other
developers can build.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:02.090
But right now,
games and smart home

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:03.740
are the big two categories.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:07.700
And one of the games that I
can also demo and show you

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.610
is called MixLab.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.680
And let me demo
this from Action.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.100
So this action
basically allows you

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:18.740
to mix different musical
instruments together just

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.540
using your voice command.

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:23.799
So (INTO SIMULATOR)
give me a dreamy guitar.

00:12:23.799 --> 00:12:28.790
[GUITAR MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:31.500
And I can add layer to it.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.250
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.710
MANDY CHAN: So this is just
one of the many ways that--

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:39.440
the creativity and the
imagination is unlimited.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:43.200
It's all up to developers and
I'm really looking forward

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:46.920
to what the crazy, amazing
stuff that the developers can

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.079
do with our platform.

00:12:48.079 --> 00:12:50.370
MELANIE WARRICK: What's
something you've built with it?

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:51.570
MANDY CHAN: Good question.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:54.750
So the first voice
application I built

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:56.520
is called [INAUDIBLE] Speech.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:00.390
And I'm a strong
believer to build

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:03.210
applications that help people.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:06.600
And [INAUDIBLE] Speech
is a voice application

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.230
that helps people who speak
English as a second language

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:12.420
to improve their pronunciation.

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:15.210
Because I was born and
raised in Hong Kong,

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:17.490
English is my second language.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:21.330
And growing up, I had
this electronic dictionary

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:24.270
to learn how to speak English.

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.400
And when I discovered
smart assistant,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.230
it's like an entire
new world to me.

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:31.200
I have the environments
here-- every day

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:32.990
I'm forced to speak English.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:36.000
For people overseas, they
don't have the environments.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.580
So think about how much we can
do for people overseas if we

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.820
have more educational actions.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.680
They can use this to
help their children

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.860
or help people who have autism--

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:53.190
different ways that people
can use smart assistant

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:55.830
to learn different things.

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:58.009
MELANIE WARRICK: I like
that point just because--

00:13:58.009 --> 00:14:00.300
I remember when I was trying
to learn another language.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:03.420
And when you don't have someone
to engage with who also--

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:07.136
and even if you did, if
they make you feel small

00:14:07.136 --> 00:14:08.760
when you're trying
to engage with them,

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:10.530
it makes you less
interested in continuing

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:12.104
to pursue that language.

00:14:12.104 --> 00:14:13.770
And that's why they
keep saying children

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:16.580
can learn more easily
in other languages

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:18.570
just because people are
more patient typically.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:21.600
Technology or a
computer, it doesn't

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:23.030
have emotions in the game.

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:25.020
That's so great that
that's what you worked on.

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:25.770
MANDY CHAN: Yes.

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:30.900
I still want to see more
actions that focus on this area.

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:33.490
Google focused on the
system, get things done,

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:35.760
helping people make their
lives more productive.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.860
I believe that education is the
untapped area for this space.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:42.100
MARK MANDEL: That's amazing.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.731
So from a developer
perspective, if somebody

00:14:44.731 --> 00:14:46.230
is like, oh, this
sounds really cool

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.630
and I want to build an
action, what tools--

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:51.190
what stuff have we got
available for them to do that?

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:53.950
MANDY CHAN: So we have Action
on Google Platform, that's

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.160
the place that you
should go first.

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.500
And also, as I
mentioned earlier,

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.830
our team recently just
published two Codelabs.

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:06.540
Level one and level two is
absolutely beginner level.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:10.830
You can quickly start building
Action on Google from scratch.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.710
And Dialogflow is
another platform

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:17.640
that helps people who might
not even know how to code--

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:20.160
that you can build
an action quickly.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:22.860
So let me explain how
Dialogflow and Action on Google

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:23.550
work together.

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:24.633
MARK MANDEL: Sounds great.

00:15:24.633 --> 00:15:27.900
MANDY CHAN: So Dialogflow
provides a intuitive graphical

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:31.320
user interface to create
conversational experience.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.410
You program in few example
sentences of things

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:37.050
that a user might
speak, and then you

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:41.460
can also specify what values
you need to get from the user.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:45.900
It then uses machine learning
to understand the sentences

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:47.790
and manage the conversation.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.890
The key part here is
that you no longer

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:53.120
need to process the
raw strings, Dialogflow

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:54.750
will handle that for you.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:59.050
Basically all the heavy lifting,
Dialogflow does it all for you.

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:03.600
So that's how Action on Google
and Dialogflow work together.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:05.760
And other than
that, our team also

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:09.270
created some amazing
templates for people

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:13.470
to build Action on Google
without coding experience.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:18.090
In fact, at I/O when I
gave a demo at the Sandbox,

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:19.470
I met a couple of teachers.

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:22.230
They stopped by the booth,
and they were so excited

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:25.050
that I can create
tools for my students

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.900
to learn interactively
by using Action

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:29.414
on Google and Dialogflow.

00:16:29.414 --> 00:16:30.330
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:31.710
MARK MANDEL: If I--

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:35.065
say I'm a programmer and I
want to create an action.

00:16:35.065 --> 00:16:37.860
As human, I'm going to code
something to make that happen.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:41.070
And then how do I make that code
do a thing, like, I don't know,

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:41.940
turn on my lights--

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:42.300
[LIGHT SWITCH CLICK]

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:43.675
--or open my garage
or something?

00:16:43.675 --> 00:16:44.980
[GARAGE OPENS]

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:49.080
MANDY CHAN: So for devices we
have this Google Assistant SDK.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.970
But for voice
application that we

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:55.480
can use with the Google
Home, the speaker alone,

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.880
as well as the phone,
we have this Action

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:59.800
on Google client library.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:04.420
So I recommend developers go
to our documentation's page

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:09.010
to learn all the new
terminologies and concepts,

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:11.500
and how to create
your own fulfillments.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:14.980
The fulfillment basically
is the business logic

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:17.357
that your actions can
do, can get things done.

00:17:17.357 --> 00:17:18.940
MARK MANDEL: There's
some connections,

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:21.400
I think, to Firebase functions
or something like that.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:22.180
MANDY CHAN: Yes.

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:22.679
Yes.

00:17:22.679 --> 00:17:24.619
So great questions.

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:29.710
So our team highly recommend
developers to use Firebase.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:32.800
Basically you just run the
command line, firebase init,

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:36.160
and then you can use the
function, the Firestore,

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:38.710
the database, and hosting--

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:40.469
everything in one place.

00:17:40.469 --> 00:17:42.135
You can create your
action very quickly.

00:17:42.135 --> 00:17:42.410
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:44.380
And there's integration
there between actions and--

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:45.550
MANDY CHAN: And Firebase, yes.

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:46.420
MARK MANDEL: Lovely.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:49.090
MELANIE WARRICK: Where
do you see SSML going?

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:51.460
MANDY CHAN: SSML is a standard.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.950
And it means that there's
multiple communities

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:57.110
or companies, they have
to agree on this standard.

00:17:57.110 --> 00:17:59.410
And I think the
direction right now

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:03.970
is really going to where the
synthesized voice will become

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:06.960
as good as professional actors.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.350
Like, at Google I/O we announced
that there are six voices

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:13.600
coming up for Google Assistant.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.300
And Google, we have
[INAUDIBLE] that

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:19.640
can create almost
human-like voices.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:22.540
So this is where I
see that maybe soon we

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.000
will replace the
actor and actress,

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:27.130
and the computer can
just speak like us.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:31.330
And another thing I really
like to think of is emotion.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:34.870
I mentioned earlier, this is a
new way we can create software.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:37.190
And this is also the time
that we can give software

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:39.640
a personality by using SSML.

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:43.102
And this is where I see that
the direction is going that way.

00:18:43.102 --> 00:18:45.310
MELANIE WARRICK: And so
having the emotion to make it

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:47.950
seem more authentic and
more real, and closer

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:49.002
to human-like in a way.

00:18:49.002 --> 00:18:49.710
MANDY CHAN: Yeah.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:52.540
Oh, and one more thing
I want to highlight is--

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:56.320
I mentioned SSML is standard,
and at Google we really think

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:59.830
outside of the box,
that standards are just

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:03.820
common text that different
platforms can use,

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:06.790
such just break
[INAUDIBLE] But we

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:09.950
think outside of the box to
create some customized SSML,

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:12.830
such as Media and Powertech.

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.580
This is unique to Action
on Google Platform

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:17.630
and nobody else has that.

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:21.760
And the Media tech allows
you to compose sounds

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:23.740
parallely or sequentially.

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:24.760
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:26.676
MANDY CHAN: I think I
gave a presentation back

00:19:26.676 --> 00:19:29.650
in December 2017 in New York.

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:32.780
I'm not a musician myself,
I would love to be.

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:36.490
But SSML and this whole
voice applications space

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:39.330
allows me to think
like I'm a composer,

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:43.120
I'm a sound designer, that I
can make something really fun.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:44.244
And people are using that.

00:19:44.244 --> 00:19:46.660
MELANIE WARRICK: Sound designer
with voice, sound designer

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:49.994
with music, with different
types of various sounds

00:19:49.994 --> 00:19:51.910
that you have in the
background type of thing.

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:53.290
MANDY CHAN: Yes, yes.

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:57.100
MELANIE WARRICK: So I know
one of the push backs from I/O

00:19:57.100 --> 00:20:00.490
was that there was concerns
about people having Assistant

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:03.400
call up and talk to
them, and whether they'd

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:05.860
be able to recognize that
it's a person or not.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:08.686
I saw some feedback
that was saying,

00:20:08.686 --> 00:20:10.810
we'll make sure it identifies
itself as a computer.

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:13.960
Do you have an opinion
on this realistic voice

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:16.270
being used from the computer?

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:17.950
MANDY CHAN: Like any
new technologies,

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:20.980
it takes time for people
to get used to it.

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:26.000
I think personally, I'm very
open-minded and creative.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.345
So I understand why
people are concerned,

00:20:28.345 --> 00:20:32.740
the ethical about turning
a robot into a human.

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:35.340
For me, I'm a big
fan for sci-fi.

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:39.220
I watched the movie,
"Her" and "Star Trek"

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:40.330
is my favorite TV shows.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:41.330
MELANIE WARRICK: Me too.

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:43.150
MANDY CHAN: So I'm
not against that,

00:20:43.150 --> 00:20:46.570
but I can also understand why
people have those concerns.

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:50.080
And this is a really big moment.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.460
Because in the past--

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:53.330
I like to use these example.

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:59.370
The TV, back when you were young
you have to go up to the TV

00:20:59.370 --> 00:20:59.870
and turn--

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.050
MARK MANDEL: How old
do you think I am?

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:03.930
MANDY CHAN: I am
really bad at telling.

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:04.940
MELANIE WARRICK: I
was going to say,

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:07.481
if you're talking about the days
when we didn't have remotes,

00:21:07.481 --> 00:21:08.860
yeah, I remember those days.

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:10.860
MANDY CHAN: Yeah, those
day without the remotes.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:14.080
You have to go up to the
TV and turn on the knob.

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:15.665
And then we have remotes--

00:21:15.665 --> 00:21:17.290
MELANIE WARRICK: And
that was a luxury.

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:18.580
MANDY CHAN: Yes.

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:21.040
And now we don't
even need to have

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:25.520
any physical button and
remote, we can use our voice.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:27.660
So this is really a
big, big time for us.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:28.660
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:29.580
Agreed.

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:30.385
MARK MANDEL: So when
am I going to be

00:21:30.385 --> 00:21:33.010
able to walk into my house and
be like, "Turn on the lights"

00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:34.060
and everything will work?

00:21:34.060 --> 00:21:35.434
And I'll be like,
"Start dinner."

00:21:35.434 --> 00:21:38.780
MANDY CHAN: You can do it
now with the lights part.

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:42.289
The dinner part,
I'm not quite sure.

00:21:42.289 --> 00:21:43.830
MELANIE WARRICK:
We're not there yet.

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:45.440
But you might be able to
talk to your Assistant

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:46.550
like they're your buddy.

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:47.030
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:49.340
MELANIE WARRICK: I think there's
some examples of that out there

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:49.625
actually.

00:21:49.625 --> 00:21:50.940
MARK MANDEL: Who
can talk back to me?

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:51.170
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:52.310
MARK MANDEL: So you
mentioned this actually

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:53.540
a little bit before
where you were talking

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:55.190
about Google Assistant
and then also

00:21:55.190 --> 00:21:57.920
the fact that you could do
voice actions on the phone.

00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:00.253
Do you see any difference
between those two, or at least

00:22:00.253 --> 00:22:03.200
even how they're used or
just that whole environment?

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:04.070
MANDY CHAN: Yes.

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:08.780
So building application
for Action on Google Home

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:11.360
specifically, there's no screen.

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:13.940
And when we build
voice applications

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:18.050
we really have to think about
the dialogue, the conversation.

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:20.870
It's like you are
a script writer.

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:24.260
But when you build for mobile
apps or web developments

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:27.560
you don't need to think about
all this storytelling, how

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:31.350
you interact with
another smart assistant.

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:34.880
And another thing
is SSML, of course.

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:38.830
And basically, the UX and UI--

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:42.050
I like to think that
the conversation flow is

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:44.810
like the user
interface, but SSML

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.060
is like the user experience.

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:49.130
Because who are you talking to?

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:51.020
The personality
that you're creating

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:52.820
for your voice applications.

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:57.240
And one additional point about
building voice application.

00:22:57.240 --> 00:22:59.150
Why is that so different?

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.340
Everything is top level.

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:04.490
At any point of the
dialogue you have

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:08.270
with the assistant you don't
need to click the mobile

00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:11.150
or with the browser the
next page or going back

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:12.920
to previous page.

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:14.990
You can ask anything,
say anything

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:17.970
at any point of the time.

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:19.220
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:22.040
We talked a little bit already
