00:25:04.280 --> 00:25:06.140
to make that a bit easier maybe?

00:25:06.140 --> 00:25:10.220
MANDY CHAN: Yes, there's a
game where Dialogflow sharing.

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:14.120
So they have a lot of
interesting and powerful

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:16.580
concepts when it
comes to building

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:18.650
a conversational experience.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:21.290
And in fact, the concept
is called Context.

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:23.030
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:23.720
MANDY CHAN: Yes.

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:27.300
So you're right that when
we use voice application,

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:29.000
it will be nice
that they don't have

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.910
to keep asking you
what are the artists

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:33.280
that you just played the music.

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:35.540
If you listen to one
song and you want to say,

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:40.700
"More", the assistant knows that
you already chose John Legend.

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:42.380
And when you say,
"Play more," it

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:44.270
will just play more of
John Legend's music.

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:46.910
And that concept
is called Context.

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:51.950
And another interesting
concept is called Lifespan

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:54.320
that Dialogflow provides.

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:59.030
And I like to describe
lifespan like us,

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:01.220
we have short-term memory.

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:04.370
In a conversation
there's multiple turns.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:09.590
Back and forth for five
lifespan means five turns.

00:26:09.590 --> 00:26:12.860
If we keep talking about
some random things,

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:16.880
then the lifespan will expire
like our short-term memory.

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:19.730
We can keep talking
about something random

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:21.270
and just lose focus.

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.680
So this concept is really
powerful in voice applications

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:26.990
to give Assistant
short-term memory.

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:28.600
MARK MANDEL: That's super cool.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:31.292
There are so many layers
to just voice conversations

00:26:31.292 --> 00:26:32.750
